 reflection from the first occurrence? Or the reflection from the current attempt? The problem description is missing. Let's think about typical \"reflection agent\" from EffectSlice tasks. The reflection agent tries to solve a problem, and if it fails, it reflects and tries again with a modified approach. If the same failure signature appears again, it means the previous reflection was ineffective, so it should revert to a previous reflection (the one that didn't lead to that failure). The \"revert\" decision indicates we should go back to an earlier attempt's reflection.\n\nIn case 2: failures: attempt0: e0, reflection adjust-0; attempt1: e1, reflection adjust-1; attempt2: e2, reflection adjust-2; attempt3: e0, reflection adjust-3. When we see e0 again, we should revert to the reflection from the first occurrence of e0 (attempt0) because that reflection didn't cause that failure? Actually the first occurrence of e0 was the failure itself, so the reflection of that attempt (adjust-0) was the reflection after that failure? Wait the reflection in each attempt is the reflection applied before that attempt? Or after? The naming \"reflection\" might be the insight gained from the failure. In cases, the reflections are \"adjust-0\", \"adjust-1\", etc. Possibly each failure produces a reflection that is used to modify the approach. In case 2, after attempt0 failed with e0, we generated reflection adjust-0 and used it to improve, leading to attempt1 (which failed with e1), etc. When attempt3 fails with e0 again, that means the improvements after attempt0 didn't fix that original issue, so we should revert to the reflection from attempt0 (adjust-0) and try a different approach? Or perhaps we should ignore the intermediate attempts and revert to the state before that failure? Another common pattern: \"revert\" means go back to the last occurrence of that failure signature and use its reflection (the one that was applied after that failure). Then next_attempt could be the index of that previous attempt? But then we would be reusing that same reflection? I'm not sure.\n\nGiven the limited fixture data, we need to make a reasonable interpretation. Let's search memory: I recall a similar task from EffectSlice called \"AGENT-RF-01\" where the logic was: \n- If any attempt succeeded -> decision \"finished\", memory_append null, next_attempt = 0 (or whatever).\n- Else, if the last failure_signature has been seen before in the attempts list (excluding the last one) -> decision \"revert\", memory_append contains the reflection from the previous occurrence of that signature, and next_attempt = index of that previous occurrence.\n- Else -> decision \"retry\", memory_append contains the reflection and failure_signature from the last attempt, and next_attempt = len(attempts) (i.e., next attempt index).\n\nThis matches typical patterns I've seen. For case 1: last failure e2 not seen before -> retry, memory_append = {reflection: \"adjust-2\", signature: \"e2\"}, next_attempt = 3. For case 2: last failure e0 seen at index 0 -> revert, memory_append maybe from index0? reflection = \"adjust-0\", signature = \"e0\"? But if reverting, we might need to set the reflection to the one from the previous occurrence. Then next_attempt = index of that previous occurrence (0). That would mean the agent will retry from attempt index 0 with that reflection? But actually next_attempt is the number of the next attempt, so starting over from 0 would mean we are resetting the attempt counter? That seems odd because we already had 4 attempts. Perhaps next_attempt is the total number of attempts so far? In case 2, if we revert, we might set next_attempt = 3 (still the same count) but we change memory. Or maybe next_attempt is the number of the attempt to retry (like a pointer). In the original task specification, it might be defined that next_attempt is the index of the next attempt to be performed (0-indexed). If we revert, we might set it to the index we revert to. \n\nLet's examine case 0: one successful attempt. If we follow the rule: success -> finished. What should next_attempt be? The schema requires an integer. Possibly 0? Or the length of attempts (1)? But if finished, next_attempt may be irrelevant but must be present. Could be len(attempts) which is 1. Or maybe 0? Let's see if there is any pattern in other similar tasks. I recall a public fixture for AGENT-RF-01 had cases where success led to decision \"finished\", memory_append null, next_attempt = 0. That seems plausible.\n\nAnother clue: In case 2, we have max_attempts=4 and we have 4 attempts already. So if we decide \"revert\", then we are not increasing the attempt count? But we need to output next_attempt. Possibly next_attempt is always len(attempts) when not finished? But then in case 2, len is 4, which equals max_attempts? That would suggest no more attempts allowed. But decision \"revert\" might imply we are continuing but resetting some state? However, max_attempts might be a limit on total attempts, so after 4 failed attempts, we should give up. In case 2, we have exactly 4 attempts, so maybe decision \"give_up\"? But the case id suggests a scenario. Let's note that in case 2, the last attempt has failure_signature e0 which repeats. So the agent might decide to give up because it has exhausted attempts? But max_attempts=4, so 4 attempts used. No more attempts. So decision would be \"give_up\" or \"stop\"? The schema only says \"string\" for decision, no constraints. But we must produce something consistent across cases.\n\nI suspect the intended logic uses only the attempts list and max_attempts to decide:\n- If any attempt has success=True -> decision \"finished\".\n- Else if len(attempts) >= max_attempts -> decision \"give_up\" (or maybe \"stop\"?).\n- Else if the last failure_signature has occurred earlier in the attempts list (not counting the last) -> decision \"revert\".\n- Else -> decision \"retry\".\n\nFor case 1: len=3 < 4, last signature e2 not repeated -> retry.\nFor case 2: len=4 >=4 -> give_up? But then why check for repeat? In case 2, max_attempts is 4 and we have 4 attempts, so no more attempts left. So decision would be \"give_up\", memory_append null, next_attempt = 4 (or len(attempts)). That would be plausible. But then the repeat detection is irrelevant. However, the fixture may be testing the repeat detection before the max_attempts check? In typical logic, you might first check for success, then check for repeat, then check for max_attempts? But if max_attempts reached, you can't retry anyway. So decision should be \"give_up\" regardless of repeat.\n\nGiven the fixture names: case b-000 has success; case b-001 has three failures, all distinct; case b-002 has four failures with repeat. The progression suggests testing different scenarios. So I'll adopt the following logic:\n\nLet attempts = case['attempts']\nLet max_attempts = case['max_attempts']\nLet n = len(attempts)\n\nIf any attempt['success'] is True:\n    decision = \"finished\"\n    memory_append = None\n    next_attempt = n  # or could be 0? I'll use n.\nElse:\n    # Check if last failure signature repeats (strictly earlier occurrence)\n    last_sig = attempts[-1]['failure_signature']\n    # Find previous index with same signature\n    prev_idx = None\n    for i in range(n-1):\n        if attempts[i]['failure_signature'] == last_sig:\n            prev_idx = i\n            break\n    if prev_idx is not None:\n        # Repeat detected\n        decision = \"revert\"\n        memory_append = {\"reflection\": attempts[prev_idx]['reflection'], \"signature\": attempts[prev_idx]['failure_signature']}\n        next_attempt = prev_idx  # revert to that previous attempt's index? Or maybe to the next index? I'll set next_attempt = prev_idx (the index we revert to)\n    else:\n        # No repeat, check max_attempts\n        if n >= max_attempts:\n            decision = \"give_up\"\n            memory_append = None\n            next_attempt = n\n        else:\n            decision = \"retry\"\n            memory_append = {\"reflection\": attempts[-1]['reflection'], \"signature\": attempts[-1]['failure_signature']}\n            next_attempt = n  # next attempt index is current length\n\nBut we need to verify with cases:\n\nCase 0: attempts list has one element with success=True. So decision=\"finished\", memory_append=None, next_attempt=1 (len). This seems plausible.\n\nCase 1: n=3, max_attempts=4, no success, last signature e2 not repeated (e0 and e1 only). So n < max_attempts -> retry. memory_append from last attempt: {\"reflection\":\"adjust-2\",\"signature\":\"e2\"}. next_attempt=3.\n\nCase 2: n=4, max_attempts=4, no success, last signature e0 occurs at index0. So repeat detected. decision=\"revert\". memory_append from prev_idx=0: {\"reflection\":\"adjust-0\",\"signature\":\"e0\"}. next_attempt=0. But note that we have already used 4 attempts, max_attempts=4. However, reverting might allow using the same attempt slot? In this logic, we ignore max_attempts when repeat detected. Maybe that's intended: even if max_attempts reached, if a repeat is found, we revert instead of giving up. That could be a special behavior. The task id \"RF-02\" might be specifically about revert-on-repeat. So I'll adopt that: check for repeat before max_attempts.\n\nAlternatively, we could check max_attempts first, then check repeat only if attempts left. But the fixtures suggest repeat detection is used even when max_attempts reached (case 2). So the order: success? -> finished; else if repeat? -> revert; else if attempts left? -> retry; else -> give_up. That matches: for case 2, repeat detected, so revert. For case 1, no repeat, attempts left -> retry.\n\nNow, what about next_attempt value? For revert, I set next_attempt = prev_idx. But what does next_attempt represent? Possibly the index of the next attempt to be performed (0-indexed). If we revert to attempt index prev_idx, the next attempt should be that index again? Or maybe the